 the airport  in the days since the desperation to  escape has not waned it's just that many  people are hiding  this is a hard question to ask it'll  probably be a hard question to answer  what is it like for you sitting in dc  don't ask  seven hundred afghans are now on sanam  narogi and erlini's rescue list they're  doctors lawyers judges  journalists people associated with their  organization which help shape and  promote peace and security policies  you know  i have people calling oh  they're so nice they're so polite  um  and their lives are at risk and they're  asking us to help them the anguish is  shared by so many aid groups and  organizations scrambling to find ways to  get their contacts out of the country or  at least keep them safely hidden away  but it's not easy and government help is  required i want to be able to say  that i did what i could  and i wish other people would just think  that wherever they're sitting what did  you you know here's a moment what did  you do the taliban has vowed to respect  women's rights and forgive those who  have fought against them  the best many can hope for right now  is that the taliban's word has value  crystal gomanson global news london  still ahead fighting hatred in canada  where islamophobia is rising  in 2020 reports of racially motivated  hate crimes surged by 30 percent and  muslim canadians are one of the most  targeted groups global's morgan black  spoke to three generations who say  islamophobia is getting worse  amal mahamud is a young black muslim  canadian she knows that makes her a  target but she feels it also makes her  powerful i am not ashamed of any part of  my identity i own all three of them  her generation has a new voice through  social media they participate in social  movements they challenge hate me wearing  the hijab plays a huge part in my life  it's a part of my identity and  i don't see myself removing it for  anyone just so they could feel more  comfortable but hate is still happening  the national canadian council of muslims  has been tracking reported anti-muslim  incidents over the past decade i feel  like the attacks have always been there  it's just that more people are vocal  about it now at least nine incidents in  the last nine months have been reported  in the edmonton area disturbing physical  and verbal assaults directed mainly at  black muslim women wearing a hijab or  burka  [Music]  last december there was a string of  assaults within just days of one another  in june a new level of horror to  comprehend four members of the afsal  family were killed while taking an  evening walk in london ontario  deliberately run down with a truck a  nine-year-old boy was the only survivor  his parents sister and grandmother  murdered this is my home end of the day  i shouldn't be feeling this way  edmonton's muslim community has deep  roots in the city it's home to canada's  first mosque opening in 1938 harriet  shaban says young muslims today should  not be made to feel like outcasts i've  been out and i've seen  girls wearing hijab i don't know them  and i've seen people yell at them  it makes me very angry schaben remembers  a city that welcomed her in the 1960s  and the pride she took being part of a  community that dedicated itself to  giving back and becoming an essential  part of edmonton's fabric maybe if like  i said if people  understood it better maybe they would  feel differently  six in ten canadians see racism as a  serious problem facing the country  according to ipsos polling done for  global news it's a double-digit increase  from the end of 2019. black indigenous  and people of color are more likely to  say racism is one of the most serious  problems facing the country and a  growing number of canadians believe  racism has increased in their community  over the past five years we can shift  perspectives and  learn lean towards understanding one  another rather than otherizing senate  atek omar spent her own childhood  immersed in community work she says her  sons will feel that same closeness to  the mosque and to edmonton it doesn't  have to be this or that you can be  muslim and canadian and pakistani and  all of those beautiful things in one  generations of muslims have grown up in  canada or come here to better both  themselves and their communities these  three edmontonians are proof that same  sentiment continues today i feel like  the generation of the past would be so  proud of our generation today  just seeing us you know just stand  strong no matter what happens we keep  moving forward forward into a future  they hope can be free of hate morgan  black global news  go canada up next a quebec teen's  stunning win over a world champ  it was an upset for the ages at the u.s  open canadian leila fernandes eliminated  number three seed and two-time defending  champion naomi osaka during this third  round the 18 year old from laval quebec  pulled off a shocker and advances to her  first grand slam ever in her career  fernandez says she wanted to put on a  show and promises to do it again when  she hits the court tomorrow  well done and team canada gets another  record-breaking gold at the tokyo  paralympics runner nate reich sped past  the competition in his paralympic debut  racing to gold in the men's t-38 1500  meter final he also set a new record for  the event finishing at three minutes and  58.92 seconds the 26 year old american  canadian has broken multiple records in  international competition his victory  marks canada's fifth gold of the games  raising team canada's total medal count  to 21.  well done that is global national for  this saturday night i'm robin gill  tonight your canada is this field of  sunflowers at sultan farm and sanctuary  in ottawa we'd love to see your corner  of the country so please keep sending  your photos to viewers at  thank you for watching and i'll see you  back here tomorrow have a great night  [Music]  you
 

